  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "As is detailed in Chapters 7 and 17, the slope $ b_{op} $ of the parent-offspring regression provides information on genetic variance components. What is the power of such parent-offspring regressions? We first note that N data points are used to compute the regression. For a single-parent regression, N is the number of parents, while for a midparent regression, N is the number of pairs of parents (we distinguish between these by indexing the regressions by p and $ \\bar{p} $, respectively). For each parental point, the offspring value could be that for a single offspring (n = 1) or the mean value of n offspring (indexed by o and $ \\bar{o} $, respectively).",
      "source_page": 886,
      "printed_page": 866,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_appendix1:p068:b2",
        "Genetics_appendix1:p068:b3"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        134.0,
        178.0,
        1093.0,
        433.0
      ],
      "source_pages": [
        886
      ],
