    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "where $ r_{op} $ is the parent-offspring correlation. The sampling variance under the null hypothesis that $ b_{op} = 0 $ follows by setting $ r_{op} = 0 $. If the parent-offspring regression has true slope $ b_{op} $, what sample size (measured by number of parents) is required for a test to have power $ 1 - \\beta $? Since this is a one-sided test ( $ b_{op} = 0 $ vs. $ b_{op} > 0 $), substituting Equation A5.9 into Equation A5.4c (and hence assuming that the variances under null and alternative hypotheses are essentially equal, i.e., $ \\sigma_{0}^{2} \\simeq \\sigma_{1}^{2} $) gives",
      "source_page": 886,
      "printed_page": 866,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_appendix1:p068:b7"
      ],
      "bbox": [
